<title>NORWAY: Norway April oil output hits 3.28 mln bpd - survey.</title>
<headline>Norway April oil output hits 3.28 mln bpd - survey.</headline>
<dateline>OSLO 1997-05-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Norway pumped more than 3.28 million barrels per day (bpd) of crude in April, a rise of nine percent from a month earlier, a Reuter survey of oil companies operating in Norwegian waters showed.</p>
<p>The April figure does not include 152,564 bpd of condensate output from the North Sea Sleipner area.</p>
<p>In March, the Scandinavian country produced 3.01 million bpd as a series of weather-related delays to offshore loadings caused sporadic shutdowns to production.</p>
<p>Output was higher at all major developments on the Norwegian continental shelf -- Statfjord and nearby satellites, Gullfaks, Oseberg and Ekofisk.</p>
<p>But Saga Petroleum suffered a downturn at both its Tordis and Vigdis fields.</p>
<p>A maintenance shutdown at Tordis for the last four days of April brought the field's average throughput down to 59,840 bpd compared with a normal level of 75,000 bpd.</p>
<p>Vigdis was shut in on April 15 due to problems with the gas compressor. The field, which came on stream in January five months ahead of schedule, was at around 31,450 bpd before the closure.</p>
<p>A spokeswoman for Vigdis operator, Saga Petroleum ASA, said the company hoped to restart output this week.</p>
